<section>
  <p class="kicker">For ADS-L conformity</p>
  <h1>The declaration skyBlip Go and Go+ need, and the documents that go with it.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    ADS-L has no ETSO and no MOPS. Under <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.A.4</strong>
    the manufacturer verifies its equipment against the specification and
    declares conformity on the template in the specification&rsquo;s appendix.
    Under <strong>A.5</strong> it ships four documents with the product: that
    declaration, the declaration of conformity behind the CE marking, an
    installation manual and a user manual.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page holds the declaration for skyBlip Go and Go+, filled in as far as
    the facts allow, and links each of the four documents. It will be signed
    in November or December 2026. Two of the four statements the manufacturer
    must make do not hold yet, and the tables below say which.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Go and Go+</h2>
  <h3>One product for ADS-L: a Go+ is a Go with accessories.</h3>

  <p>
    A skyBlip Go runs on a LilyGO T-Echo, and a skyBlip Go+ on a LilyGO T-Echo
    Plus. Everything ADS-L depends on is the same in both: the nRF52840
    processor, the Quectel L76K GNSS receiver and its PPS, the Semtech SX1262
    868&nbsp;MHz radio and the antenna. Both run the same firmware image, built
    for one board definition. The T-Echo Plus adds an inertial unit, a buzzer,
    a vibration motor and a larger battery. None of them transmits, receives
    or feeds the ADS-L payload, so one declaration covers both under one part
    number.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The documents</h2>
  <h3>What A.5 asks a manufacturer to ship, and where each one is.</h3>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Document</th><th>Device</th><th>Where it stands</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>ADS-L declaration of conformity</th>
          <td>Go, Go+</td>
          <td>drafted <a href="#declaration">below</a>, not signed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>CE: radio, EMC, safety, health</th>
          <td>Go, a LilyGO T-Echo</td>
          <td>
            <a href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0617/7190/7253/files/T-ECHO_CE.pdf?v=1691478157">attestation of conformity SZ1220803-35313E-RF</a>,
            issued by BACL Shenzhen on 18 Nov 2022 to LilyGO for model T-Echo,
            under the Radio Equipment Directive 2014/53/EU. It is a test
            lab&rsquo;s attestation, not the manufacturer&rsquo;s declaration.
            LilyGO lists it on
            <a href="https://lilygo.cc/pages/certifications">lilygo.cc/pages/certifications</a>,
            beside the T-Echo&rsquo;s FCC and MIC certificates.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>CE: radio, EMC, safety, health</th>
          <td>Go+, a LilyGO T-Echo Plus</td>
          <td>missing. The radio is the T-Echo&rsquo;s, but LilyGO sells the T-Echo Plus as its own model, and the T-Echo attestation names model T-Echo only. LilyGO publishes none for the T-Echo Plus.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Installation manual</th>
          <td>Go, Go+</td>
          <td>not written. It has to cover where to mount the device and its antenna for range, vertical polarisation and an omnidirectional pattern.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>User manual</th>
          <td>Go, Go+</td>
          <td>
            the <%= link_to_page "guides", label: "guides" %>. The ADS-L parts
